   0 # Architecture Standards: Result Object Pattern
   1 
   2 This document outlines the standard for error handling and state management across the backend modules using the **Result Object Pattern**.
   3 
   4 ## Overview
   5 
   6 The Result Object Pattern is a functional programming pattern used to represent the outcome of an operation that can either succeed or fail. Instead of throwing exceptions for expected business failures, we return a `Result` object that contains either the success value or a domain-specific error.
   7 
   8 ### Benefits
   9 - **Explicit Error Handling**: Failures are part of the function signature, forcing callers to handle them.
  10 - **Type Safety**: TypeScript ensures that you cannot access the value of a failed operation without checking its status.
  11 - **Clean Architecture**: Domain errors are separated from infrastructure-specific exceptions (like Firestore or Vector Store errors).
  12 - **Standardized API Responses**: Standardized handlers translate Result objects into consistent JSON responses.
  13 
  14 ---
  15 
  16 ## 1. The Result Class
  17 
  18 The core implementation lives in `src/lib/utils/result.ts`.
  19 
  20 ```typescript
  21 export class Result<T, E = Error> {
  22   public readonly isSuccess: boolean;
  23   public readonly isFailure: boolean;
  24   public readonly error: E | null;
  25   private readonly _value: T | null;
  26 
  27   // ... (implementation details)
  28 
  29   public getValue(): T {
  30     if (!this.isSuccess) {
  31       throw new Error("Can't get the value of an error result.");
  32     }
  33     return this._value as T;
  34   }
  35 
  36   public static ok<T, E>(value?: T): Result<T, E> {
  37     return new Result<T, E>(true, undefined, value);
  38   }
  39 
  40   public static fail<T, E>(error: E): Result<T, E> {
  41     return new Result<T, E>(false, error);
  42   }
  43 }
  44 ```
  45 
  46 ---
  47 
  48 ## 2. Defining Domain Errors
  49 
  50 Each module should define its own domain error class and error types.
  51 
  52 **Example (`src/modules/projects/domain/errors/ProjectErrors.ts`):**
  53 
  54 ```typescript
  55 export enum ProjectErrorType {
  56   NOT_FOUND = "NOT_FOUND",
  57   VALIDATION = "VALIDATION",
  58   SAVE_ERROR = "SAVE_ERROR",
  59   INTERNAL = "INTERNAL"
  60 }
  61 
  62 export class ProjectError {
  63   constructor(
  64     public readonly type: ProjectErrorType,
  65     public readonly message: string,
  66     public readonly details?: any
  67   ) {}
  68 
  69   public static notFound(message: string): ProjectError {
  70     return new ProjectError(ProjectErrorType.NOT_FOUND, message);
  71   }
  72   // ... other static factory methods
  73 }
  74 ```
  75 
  76 ---
  77 
  78 ## 3. Implementation Layers
  79 
  80 ### Repository Layer
  81 Repositories should wrap technical exceptions and return Result objects.
  82 
  83 ```typescript
  84 async getById(id: string): Promise<Result<Project, ProjectError>> {
  85     try {
  86         const doc = await this.collection.doc(id).get();
  87         if (!doc.exists) {
  88             return Result.fail(ProjectError.notFound(`Project ${id} not found`));
  89         }
  90         return Result.ok(new Project(doc.data()));
  91     } catch (error: any) {
  92         return Result.fail(ProjectError.internal(error.message));
  93     }
  94 }
  95 ```
  96 
  97 ### Use Case Layer
  98 Use cases propagate results or combine multiple results.
  99 
 100 ```typescript
 101 async execute(id: string): Promise<Result<Project, ProjectError>> {
 102     return this.projectRepository.getById(id);
 103 }
 104 ```
 105 
 106 ### Controller Layer
 107 Controllers handle side effects (like cache revalidation) and return the Result.
 108 
 109 ```typescript
 110 async saveProject(data: any, userId: string) {
 111     const result = await this.saveProjectUseCase.execute(data, userId);
 112     if (result.isSuccess) {
 113         revalidatePath('/dashboard');
 114     }
 115     return result;
 116 }
 117 ```
 118 
 119 ---
 120 
 121 ## 4. API Integration
 122 
 123 API routes use standardized handlers from `src/lib/utils/api-utils.ts` to convert Results into standard HTTP responses.
 124 
 125 ```typescript
 126 export const GET = withAuth(async ({ params }) => {
 127     const controller = container.resolve(ProjectController);
 128     const result = await controller.getProjectById(params.id);
 129     
 130     // Standardized mapping: NOT_FOUND -> 404, UNAUTHORIZED -> 401, etc.
 131     return handleProjectResult(result);
 132 });
 133 ```
 134 
 135 ---
 136 
 137 ## 5. Summary of Standard Handlers
 138 
 139 | Handler | Domain Error Type | Location |
 140 |---------|-------------------|----------|
 141 | `handleUserResult` | `UserError` | `api-utils.ts` |
 142 | `handleProjectResult` | `ProjectError` | `api-utils.ts` |
 143 | `handleBillingResult` | `BillingError` | `api-utils.ts` |
 144 | `handleRagResult` | `RagError` | `api-utils.ts` |
 145 | `handleTemplateResult` | `TemplateError` | `api-utils.ts` |
 146 | `handleAIContentResult` | `AIContentError` | `api-utils.ts` |
 147 
````

